{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>Let there be luminaries in the firmament of the heavens.</b> מְאֹרֹת is written deficient (חָסֵר), Rashi explained that it is an expression of מְאֵרָה (curse/affliction), etc. I say that this is why the expression denoting affliction (מוֹרָה) was mentioned specifically in connection with the luminaries — because the luminaries are the cause of time, and all things that fall under time contain affliction (צַעַר), as our Sages of blessed memory said (Megillah 10a): everywhere it says ״וַיְהִי״ (and it came to pass) it is nothing but an expression of affliction, and they conclude that specifically where it says ״וַיְהִי בִּימֵי״ (and it came to pass in the days) it is an expression of affliction. And the reason for this is that anything dependent on days — that is, on time — has affliction, whereas all the supernal beings, which are above time and over which time has no dominion, contain no affliction whatsoever. Therefore it says יְהִי מְאֹרֹת deficient, for everything under the sun has מְאֵרָה and affliction, because time consumes all. And the fact that in this section the word מָאוֹר appears five times, and likewise in the first section the word אוֹר appears five times, accords with the words of the Midrash (Genesis Rabbah 2:5), which says: ״וַיֹּאמֶר אֱלֹהִים יְהִי אוֹר״ — these are the deeds of the righteous, etc. And it wished to explain the reason for five times אוֹר, that they correspond to the five books of the Torah — that is, the deeds of the righteous."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "The word אֲרִירָה in the second clause is obscure; read contextually as equivalent to אֲרוּרָה (cursed/afflicted), matching the Rashi parallel. The phrase פֵּרֵשׁ רַשִׁ״י שֶׁהוּא לְשׁוֹן מְאֵרָה וְכוּ׳ is rendered as a summary citation of Rashi's comment without expanding the 'etc.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T23:33:20.898750981Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}